/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 486 0 R
/ActualText (Cost Savings: EVs have lower operating costs due to cheaper electricity compared to fuel and reduced maintenance needs. Government incentives, such as the FAME II subsidy and state-specific benefits, significantly lower the initial purchase cost.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 487 0 R
/ActualText (Environmental Concerns: With growing awareness of climate change, consumers are more conscious of their carbon footprint. EVs produce zero tailpipe emissions, helping to reduce air pollution, especially in urban areas.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/ActualText (Government Incentives: Tax credits and rebates lower the upfront costs of EVs, making them more financially accessible.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 489 0 R
/ActualText (Technological Advancements: Improved range \(200-300 km per charge\) and expanding charging infrastructure reduce range anxiety. Innovations in battery technology and vehicle performance make EVs more attractive.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
292 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 490 0 R
/ActualText (State-Specific Benefits: Additional financial incentives, such as registration fee waivers and road tax exemptions in states like Maharashtra, Delhi, and Gujarat, make EVs even more affordable.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
